WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.269
Transport Layer Security is one of the basic trust mechanisms behind modern web communication.

2
00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:21.981
When a browser connects to a site, an application calls an API, a mobile app talks to a backend, or one service calls another inside a cloud environment, TLS is often the protocol that provides confidentiality, integrity, and authentication.

3
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:34.133
It also appears at load balancers, API gateways, reverse proxies, SaaS platforms, ingress controllers, service meshes, and internal applications that never face the public internet.

4
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:42.647
Post-quantum cryptography matters here because TLS relies on public-key cryptography during important parts of the handshake.

5
00:00:42.647 --> 00:00:47.987
Public-key mechanisms help authenticate the server and establish key material.

6
00:00:47.987 --> 00:00:57.043
Once the handshake has done its job, TLS commonly uses symmetric encryption to protect the application data moving across the connection.

7
00:00:57.043 --> 00:01:08.498
That split is important: the quantum concern is most urgent for some public-key algorithms, while symmetric encryption and hashing have different planning considerations.

8
00:01:08.498 --> 00:01:11.207
The risk is not limited to a future outage.

9
00:01:11.207 --> 00:01:16.702
Harvest-now, decrypt-later scenarios matter when traffic is sensitive for years.

10
00:01:16.702 --> 00:01:27.306
An adversary may collect encrypted network traffic today and attempt to decrypt it later if the public-key protection used during the session becomes breakable.

11
00:01:27.306 --> 00:01:40.386
This is especially relevant for regulated information, government data, sensitive business records, intellectual property, and enterprise API traffic where confidentiality has a long lifetime.

12
00:01:40.386 --> 00:01:46.965
For TLS and web services, PQC is a migration and operations problem, not just a cryptography problem.

13
00:01:46.965 --> 00:01:59.194
The work touches endpoint inventory, certificates, libraries, clients, mobile apps, proxies, monitoring, performance, change management, vendor readiness, and rollback planning.

14
00:01:59.194 --> 00:02:12.971
A useful program treats PQC as practical crypto agility: understand where TLS is used, prioritize based on risk, test standards-based mechanisms, and make change possible without breaking critical services.
